Acute Tonsillitis
Paediatric & Neonatal
Severe sore throat, Exudative enlarged tonsils, Tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-003
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of severe sore throat, odynophagia, and high fever requiring urgent evaluation to rule out peritonsillar abscess or airway compromise.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and mother
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an inability to swallow fluids, drooling, and high fever exceeding 38.5C. Immediate assessment was required to assess airway patency, hydration status, and rule out deep neck space infection prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "It feels like swallowing glass, and I can't even drink water because it hurts so much."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a sudden onset of severe sore throat and significant difficulty swallowing (odynophagia) that commenced 12 hours ago. The patient reports profound systemic chills and a high fever. There is a complete absence of cough, rhinorrhea, or conjunctivitis. The voice has developed a muffled, thick quality over the past few hours. Referred bilateral otalgia is present.
Functional Status: Currently bedbound due to severe systemic malaise and pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Impaired oral intake raising acute dehydration risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/65 | HR: 115 | Temp: 38.9C | RR: 20 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 30 Kg
General: Toxic appearance, febrile, clear voice changes (hot potato voice), no drooling, no stridor.
Physical Exam: Oropharyngeal examination reveals markedly enlarged, visibly erythematous palatine tonsils nearly meeting in the midline. There is a patchy yellowish-white purulent exudate explicitly confined to the tonsillar crypts. The uvula is midline without deviation. Tender, noticeably swollen anterior cervical and jugulodigastric lymph nodes are palpable bilaterally. Halitosis is distinct.
Environmental Observations: Patient has a water bottle at the bedside but is refusing to drink.
Medication Reconciliation: No current medications. Nil known drug allergies.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Tonsillitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Peritonsillar Abscess - Ruled out due to the midline uvula, symmetric tonsillar enlargement, and absence of trismus.

Viral Pharyngitis - Unlikely due to the presence of high fever, purulent exudates, absence of cough, and tender anterior cervical nodes (Centor score 4).

Epiglottitis - Ruled out due to the absence of stridor, absence of drooling, and the patient's ability to tolerate a supine position.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient’s rapid onset of severe odynophagia, high fever, and muffled voice warranted immediate after-hours evaluation to exclude airway obstruction and peritonsillar abscess. Objective assessment confirmed severe, exudative acute tonsillitis fulfilling Centor criteria for bacterial etiology, while successfully ruling out surgical emergencies. Delaying care until the following day would have led to worsening dehydration due to complete fluid refusal and risking potential airway compromise. Immediate provision of targeted antimicrobial therapy and robust analgesia mitigates these risks safely in the community.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Phenoxymethylpenicillin 500mg BD for 10 days. Administered weight-based oral ibuprofen stat.
Positive Behavioral Change: Take one small sip of cold water every five minutes immediately following an analgesic dose. Rewarding the reduction of pain with the soothing sensation of cold fluids reinforces continuous hydration behavior and prevents hospitalization for intravenous fluids.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours to ensure adequate oral intake and resolution of fever.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops drooling, difficulty breathing, stridor, inability to open the mouth (trismus), or asymmetric swelling of the throat.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; clinical conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Sore throat and fever should drastically improve within 48 to 72 hours of starting antibiotics. Full course completion is required to prevent rheumatic complications.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if breathing becomes noisy or difficult, or if the patient becomes completely unable to swallow saliva.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Antibiotics are required to treat the underlying streptococcal bacterial infection, while regular pain relief is critical to allow swallowing of fluids.
Actionable Steps: Complete the entire 10-day antibiotic course even if feeling better. Use throat lozenges or ice blocks to provide topical pain relief.

